{
  "lessonActivities": [
    {
      "id": "lesson-1",
      "title": "10.3 Bare Infinitive: Perception Verbs",
      "content": "<p>Perception verbs such as see, hear, feel, watch, notice take bare infinitive with action verbs that follow (I saw him leave, I heard her sing).</p>",
      "examples": ["I saw him run.", "She heard the baby cry."],
      "difficulty": 2,
      "audio": "bare_inf_1.mp3",
      "functions": ["Recognize perception verbs","Construct bare infinitive clauses"],
      "tips": "For direct perception use bare infinitive; for indirect use gerund (I saw him running)."
    },
    {
      "id": "lesson-2",
      "title": "10.3 Bare Infinitive: Causative Verbs",
      "content": "<p>Use make, let, have in causative structures with bare infinitive (She made him apologize, He let me go, I had him check the engine).</p>",
      "examples": ["They made her finish the job.", "I let him borrow my book."],
      "difficulty": 2,
      "audio": "bare_inf_2.mp3",
      "functions": ["Use causative verbs","Compare with to-infinitive"],
      "tips": "Make means force; let means permit; have means arrange."
    },
    {
      "id": "lesson-3",
      "title": "10.3 Bare Infinitive vs To-Infinitive",
      "content": "<p>Some verbs can be followed by either bare or to-infinitive with changed meaning: help, start, begin, continue. Learn when each is correct.</p>",
      "examples": ["She helped me (to) carry the box.", "He started to run / He started running."],
      "difficulty": 3,
      "audio": "bare_inf_3.mp3",
      "functions": ["Distinguish both forms","Choose correct literal meaning"],
      "tips": "Help can use both; other verbs vary by context and emphasis."
    },
    {
      "id": "lesson-4",
      "title": "10.3 Bare Infinitive with Modals and Let/Make",
      "content": "<p>After modals and causatives we also use bare infinitive: can make/might let/may have etc. Eg: She can make them work, they might let him go.</p>",
      "examples": ["You must make her listen.", "He may let the dogs out."],
      "difficulty": 3,
      "audio": "bare_inf_4.mp3",
      "functions": ["Apply in advanced constructs","Use in complicated sentences"],
      "tips": "Linking modals and causatives keeps form compact and direct."
    },
    {
      "id": "lesson-5",
      "title": "10.3 Bare Infinitive in Conversation",
      "content": "<p>In spoken English, bare infinitive after certain verbs is very common. This lesson offers conversation patterns for all ages.</p>",
      "examples": ["Let me help you.", "She made me laugh."],
      "difficulty": 3,
      "audio": "bare_inf_5.mp3",
      "functions": ["Use in spoken contexts","Practice everyday structure"],
      "tips": "Remember bare infinitive is usual for direct verbs of influence/perception."
    }
  ],
